< \section{Programming in Assembler}
< %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
< \nsl{The programming of microcontroller is a little bit different from the programming of microprocessors. In the case of microcontroller mostly the programming is done as so called cross compiling. This means all the necessary tools for compiling are placed on a PC (editor, compiler, linker and so on). The executable files which are generated, would download to target system with a totally different processor.
< \newline
< In opposite to the cross compiling approach the development for the target itself doesn't have a download mechanism. The target processor and the processor for the development system is the same and a loader of an available operating system loads the executable and starts the program.
< \newline
< A next important difference between the developments of programs for microcontroller and microprocessor is the opportunity to use an operating system or not. In nearly every application which uses a microprocessor there is support from an operating system. Simple input/output-operations, memory allocation and mathematical libraries would be offered from the operating systems. In the case of a microcontroller you program all these functions.
< \newline
< The microcontroller CFV4e is a processor just on the border between microprocessors and microcontrollers. From the point of a programmer it is a typical microprocessor, from the point of a hardware designer it is a high sophisticated microcontroller. So in this chapter we don't find the specific restrictions which are typical for controllers. Starting with an overview about all the register (register model) from the different parts of this controller we will get more in detail about the assembler programming aspects. The focus is to understand the specific aspects of this controller not to write big assembler programs. These could be better done with C-programs.
< }
< 
< \begin{description}
< \item[{\rot\bf Register model}]~\\[-3ex]
< \bigskip
< \bi
< 	\ite the register model of all Coldfire processors has the same basis
< 	\ite it could be separated in models for the core register, supervisor register, and additional superscale units (FPU, MAC)
< 	\ite register models are important for the development of assembler programs (a C-compiler tries to hide the most aspects of registers)
< 	\ite registers are part of the core (not mapped in the address space of the memory)
< 	\bigskip
< 	\ite register model for integer operations (core register)
< 	\bi 
< 		\ite separation between data- and address-register
< 		\ite in standard transfer instruction no mix allowed (there exist special transfer instructions to move from data- to address-register or from address to data-registers)
< 		\ite the access to a data-register could be done as a Byte, Word or Longword
< 		\ite for an the access to an address-register only a Word (seldom) or Longword (standard) is possible
< 	\ei 
< \ei
