< \section{Programming in C}
< %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
< \begin{description}
< \item[{\rot\bf Specification}]~\\[-3ex]
< \bi 
< 	\ite crosscompilation
< 	\ite use a special toolchain to build programs for CF4Ve
< 	\bi 
< 		\ite compiler m68k-elf-gcc
< 		\ite version for the ``standard''-gcc (generates output the CF4e-processor)
< 		\ite in the case of crosscompilation there is no support from a operating systems
< 		\ite this means it makes no sense to use the MMU features of the processor
< 		\ite the output of the compiler depends on the position which have to be specified in the linker
< 		\ite this output format is indicated by the endings ``.bin''
< 		\ite download these progams in the target convert the ``.bin''-programs to a ``.s19''-format
< 	\ei
< 	\os{\newpage}
< 	\ite development in the target system
< 	\bi	
< 		\ite use the same compiler (m68k-elf-gcc) as in the case of a crosscompilation
< 		\ite the development have to be done in the target system (longer development cycles)
< 		\ite the executable file have to be generated position independend (controlled by a special linker command)
< 		\ite in the case of CF4e-processor the MMU is active
< 		\ite the output of the linker is build as .elf-file and could directly loaded from the operating system (elf - \underline{e}xecutable and \underline{l}inkable \underline{f}ormat) 
< 	\ei
< 	\bigskip
< 	\ite development with the same operating sytem in the target and in the cross-system
< 	\bi 
< 		\ite use uLinux as operating system for the target
< 		\ite uLinunx works with and without an MMU
< 		\ite implementation of an NFS-server (\underline{n}etwork \underline{f}ile \underline{s}ystem) on the cross-system 
< 		\ite mount the ``working directory'' of the cross-system in the target and a direct load an execute is possible
< 		\ite shorter development cycles as in the cross-compilation
< 		\ite use the advantages of an operating system
< 	\ei
< \ei
< \os{\newpage}
< \item[{\rot\bf Special features}]~\\[-3ex]
< \bi 
< 	\ite no restriction in the use of data types
< 	\ite the type float and double also supported (the basic arithmetic steps like add, sub, mul, and div directly supported from the FPU, more complex flating point arithmetic expressions like sin(x), cos(x), \begin{math} x^{y} \end{math} supported from a special library)
< 	\bigskip
< 	\ite access to registers (and memory) could be done in two different methods
< 	\bi 
< 		\ite cross-complilation without the use on the MMU
< 		\ite set a pointer to the register (type of pointer depends on the register)
< 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
< 		  \#define IPSBAR 0x1000000 & /* Base address of */ \\
< 					& /* I/O-registers */ \\
< 		  \#define PAR\_PCS3 (IPSBAR + 0xA4C) & /* Address of the specific */ \\
< 					& /* I/O */ \\
< 		    & \\
< 		  (*unsigned char *)(PAR\_PCS3)) = 0x00; & /* Set Register PAR\_PCS3 */ \\
< 					& /* to 0 */ \\
< 		   \end{tabular}
< 		\ite program runs in the ``user-mode'' of the processor
< 		\bigskip
< 		\ite development in the target with the use of the MMU
< 		\ite program have to be developed in the ``kernel-mode''
< 	\ei
< 	
< \ei
< \os{\newpage}
