> \nsl{The method of programming a microcontroller is little bit different from that used for programming of microprocessors. In the case of microcontrollers, the programming is usually done using the ‘cross compilation’ technique. In this method, all the necessary tools for compiling are installed on a PC (i.e. editor, compiler, linker etc.) and the executable file generated is then downloaded to the target system. Here, the target system (e.g. CFV4e) has a completely different processing core than the host PC (which employs e.g. an Intel Processor).
> \newline
> Another method involves development on the target directly and doesn't have a download mechanism. The target processor and the processor for the development system is the same and a loader of an available operating system loads the executable file and starts the program.
> \newline
> A next important difference between the development of programs for microcontroller and microprocessor is the opportunity to use an operating system or not. In nearly every application which uses a microprocessor there is support from an operating system (OS). Simple input/output-operations, memory allocation and mathematical libraries would be offered through the operating system. In the case of a microcontroller you program all these functions and availability of OS is not a necessity.
> \newline
> The microcontroller CFV4e is a processor just on the border between microprocessors and microcontrollers. From the point of a programmer it is a typical microprocessor, from the point of a hardware designer it is a highly sophisticated microcontroller. So in this chapter we don't find the specific restrictions which are typical for other microcontrollers. Starting with an overview about all the registers (register model) from the different parts of this controller we will progress to assembler programming aspects. The focus is to understand the specific aspects of this controller and not to write big assembler programs. These could be better done through C-programs.
> }
