> 	\ite integer and fractional operations are supported (special instructions).
> 	\ite result of EMAC instructions stored in accumulators (48 bit registers).
> 	\ite multiplication and add instruction operates in hardware (fast execution).
> \ei
> \os{\newpage}
> \begin{center}
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_2_EMAC_Integer,width=0.7\textwidth}\\
> Fig 4.2 Principle Function of an EMAC Operation on Integers
> \end{center}
> \begin{center}
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_3_EMAC_Fractional,width=0.7\textwidth}\\
> Fig 4.3 Principle Function of an EMAC Operation on Fractionals
> \end{center}
> \begin{center}
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_4_EMAC_Register,width=0.7\textwidth}\\
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_4_EMAC_ACCext01,width=0.7\textwidth}\\
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_4_EMAC_ACCext23,width=0.7\textwidth}\\
> Fig 4.4 Registers from the EMAC Unit
> \end{center}
> \os{\bigskip}
> \bi 
> 	\ite EMAC unit works with different flags.
> 	\ite the function of the EMAC unit is programmable.
> 	\ite programming steps and the flags are stored in the MACSR (MAC status register).
> 	\ite MASK register (16 bit) could be used for mask-operations (logical AND-Operation).
> \ei
> \begin{center}
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_5_MACSR,width=0.8\textwidth}\\
> Fig 4.5 Status Register (MACSR) of the EMAC Unit
> \end{center}
> \os{\bigskip}
> \bi 
> 	\ite flags according to the accumulators.
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite every accumulator has its one carry flag (PAV3 ... PAV0).
> 		\ite for all accumulators 
> 		\bi 
> 			\ite N \pr negative.
> 			\ite Z \pr zero.
> 			\ite V \pr overflow.
> 			\ite EV \pr this field is always zero  (position of the carry flag in CCR).
> 		\ei
> 	\ei
> 	\bigskip
> 	\ite operational mode field (defines the operating configuration of the EMAC unit)
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite OMC \pr overflow (1) /saturation mode (0). 
> 		\ite S/U \pr signed (1) / unsigned operations (0).
> 		\ite F/I \pr fraction (1) / integer mode (0).
> 		\ite R/T \pr round (1) / truncate mode (0).
> 	\ei
> \ei
> 
> \item[{\rot\bf FPU Registers}]~\\[-3ex]
> 
> \bi 
> 	\ite eight 64-bit floating-point data registers (FP0 - FP7).
> 	\ite one 32-bit floating-point control register (FPCR).
> 	\ite one 32-bit floating-point status register (FPSR).
> 	\ite one 32-bit floating-point instruction address register (FPIAR).
> \ei
> 
> \item[{\rot\bf Supervisor Programming Model}]~\\[-3ex]
> 
> \bi 
> 	\ite after a reset the CFV4e runs in the supervisory mode.
> 	\ite after an interrupt or an exception the CFV4e switches to supervisory mode.
> 	\ite additionally to the user registers, the supervisor has access following registers
> \ei
> \os{\newpage}
> \begin{center}
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_6_Supervisor_Registers,width=0.8\textwidth}\\
> Fig 4.6 Supervisor Programming Model
> \epsfig{file=figures/Fig_4_7_SR,width=0.8\textwidth}\\
> Fig 4.7 SR-Register
> \end{center}
> \os{\newpage}
> \bi 
> 	\ite SR \pr status register (supervisor extension of the CCR)
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite T \pr trace enable  (when T is set, the processor performs a trace exception after every instruction).
> 		\ite S \pr supervisor (1) / user state (0).
> 		\ite M \pr master/interrupt state (Cleared by an interrupt exception. It can be set by software during execution of the RTE or move to SR instructions so the OS can emulate an interrupt stack pointer).
> 		\ite I2,I1,I0 \pr interrupt priority mask (level 7 non maskable interrupt, level 1 lowest interrupt priority)
> 	\ei
> \ei
> 
> \os{\newpage}
> \item[{\rot\bf Addressing modes}]~\\[-3ex]
> \os{\bigskip}
> \bi 
> 	\ite addressing modes could be separated in five groups
> 	\bigskip
> 	\ite {\rot\bf Register addressing}
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite name of the register coded in the instruction
> 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE D0,D1 & ;transfer contents form D0 (16 bit) to D1\\
> 		      MOVE.B D1,D2 & ;transfer contents form D1 (8 bit) to D2\\
> 		      MOVE.L D1,D2 & ;transfer contents form D1 (32 bit) to D2\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVEA D0,A0 & ;transfer contents from D0 (always 32 bit)\\
> 			& ;to A0 address register involved\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVEC D1,ROMBAR0 & ;transfer contents form D1 to \\
> 			& ;Basis address of internal ROM (ROMBAR0)\\
> 			& ;supervisor instruction!!\\ 	
> 		     \end{tabular}
> 
> 	\ei
> 	\os{\newpage}
> 	\ite {\rot\bf Immediate addressing}
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite instruction contains the data
> 		\ite the immediate value is signed (signed expanded to word type (byte, word, longword)) 
> 		\ite type of addressing is useful for source addressing only
> 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE \#0x1000,D0 & ;transfer the value 1000 (hex)to D0 (16 bit)\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVEA \#0x20003000,A0 & ;transfer the value 20003000 (hex) to A0 \\
> 			& (always 32 bit)\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVE to SR \#0x1200,SR & ;transfer the value 1200 (hex) to \\
> 			& Status register SR (supervisor instruction!!)\\ 	
> 		     \end{tabular}
> 
> 	\ei
> 	\os{\newpage}
> 	\ite {\rot\bf Direct addressing}
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite instruction contains the address of a data
> 		\ite address could be specified as 16 bit address (seldom) and as 32 bit address (standard)
> 		\ite also noun as absolute addressing mode
> 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE.L(0x2000),D0 & ;load the register D0 with contents\\
> 			& of address 2000 (32 bit address)\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVEA.L(0x3000),A0 & ;load the register A0 with contents\\
> 			& of address 3000 (32 bit address)\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVE.L to SR(0x1200),SR & ;load the register SR with contents\\
> 			& of address 1200 (32 bit address)\\
> 			& (supervisor instruction!!)\\ 	
> 		     \end{tabular}
> 
> 	\ei
> 	\os{\newpage}
> 	\ite {\rot\bf Indirect addressing}
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite use one of the address register A0 - A7.
> 		\ite address register A7 is the stack pointer.
> 		\ite address registers are always 32-bit registers.
> 		\ite indirect addressing modes operate in different versions.
> 		\bigskip
> 		\ite {\rot\bf Indirect addressing standard}
> 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE (A0),D0 & ;load the register D0 with contents\\
> 			& ;of address where A0 points to\\
> 		     \end{tabular}
> 		\os{\newpage}
> 		\ite {\rot\bf Indirect addressing with predecrement / post increment}
> 		\ite pre decrement with the number of byte which will be transferred.
> 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE D0,-(A7) & ;decrement register A7 by 2 \\
> 			& ;(16 bit transfer)\\
> 			& ;load the register D0 to the \\
> 			& ;address where A1 points to\\
> 			& ;(push-operation on Intel processors)\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVEM D0..D7,-(A7) & ;generate a stack frame of all \\
> 			& ;data registers\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVEM DO..D7,A0..A7,-(A7) & ;complete stack frame
> 		     \end{tabular}
> 		\os{\newpage}
> 		\ite post increment with the number of byte which was transferred.
> 		\\\begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE (A7)+,D0 & ;load the register D0 with the \\
> 			& ;contents address where A1 points to\\
> 			& ;increment register A7 by 2 \\
> 			& ;(16 bit transfer)\\
> 			& ;(pop-operation on Intel processors)\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVEM (A7)+,D0..D7, & ;restore registers D0 - D7 \\
> 			& from stack frame\\
> 			& \\
> 		      MOVEM (A7)+, DO..D7,A0..A7 & ;restore all registers
> 		     \end{tabular}
> 		\os{\newpage}
> 		\ite {\rot\bf indirect addressing with an offset}
> 		\ite the offset is signed and a 16 bit value
> 		\\\begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE (0x10,A0),D0 & ;load the register D0 with the \\
> 			& ;contents address where (A0 + 0x10) points to\\
> 		 \end{tabular}
> 		\bigskip
> 		\ite {\rot\bf indirect addressing with an offset and a scaled index register}
> 		\ite the signed offset is an 8 bit value.
> 		\ite the index register could be one of the data or address registers.
> 		\ite the scale factor is positive and for integer operations 1, 2, 4 and for floating point operations 1, 2, 4, 8
> 		\\\begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE (0x10,A0,D0*2),D1 & ;load the register D1 with the \\
> 			& ;contents address where ((A0 + 0x10) \\
> 			& ;+ D0 * 2) points to\\
> 		 \end{tabular}
> 	\ei
> 	\os{\newpage}
> 	\ite {\rot\bf Relative addressing}
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite the program works as an address register.
> 		\ite this addressing mode has to be selected for position independent programs.
> 		\ite only useful with an offset.
> 		\ite indirect addressing modes operate in different versions.
> 		\bigskip
> 		\ite {\rot\bf Relative addressing with an offset}
> 		\ite the offset is signed and a 16 bit value.
> 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE (-100,PC),D0 & ;load the register D0 with contents\\
> 			& ;of address where (PC - 100) points to\\
> 		     \end{tabular}
> 		\bigskip
> 		\ite {\rot\bf Relative addressing with an offset and a scaled index register}
> 		\ite the signed offset is an 8 bit value.
> 		\ite the index register could be one of the data or address registers.
> 		\ite the scale factor is positive and for integer operations 1, 2, 4 and for floating point operations 1, 2, 4, 8
> 		\\\begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		      MOVE (0x10,PC,D0*2),D1 & ;load the register D1 with the \\
> 			& ;contents address where ((PC + 0x10) \\
> 			& ;+ D0 * 2) points to\\
> 		 \end{tabular}
> 	\ei
> \ei
> \os{\newpage}
> \bi 
> 	\ite Classification of the instruction set
> 	\\
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite transfer instructions
> 		\\
> 		\ite integer arithmetic instructions
> 		\\
> 		\ite floating-point arithmetic instructions
> 		\\
> 		\ite logical instructions
> 		\\
> 		\ite bit manipulation instructions
> 		\\
> 		\ite control instructions
> 		\\
> 		\ite other
> 	\ei
> \ei
> 	
> \end{description}
> 
> %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
> \section{Programming in C}
> %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
> \begin{description}
> \item[{\rot\bf Specification}]~\\[-3ex]
> \bi 
> 	\ite Cross compilation:
> 	\ite use of a special toolchain to build programs for CF4Ve
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite compiler m68k-elf-gcc.
> 		\ite version for the ``standard''-gcc (generates output for the CF4e-processor)
> 		\ite in the case of cross compilation there is no support from an operating system. This means it makes no sense to use the MMU features of the processor.
> 		\ite the output of the compiler depends on the position which has to be specified in the linker.
> 		\ite this output format is indicated by the endings ``.bin''.
> 		\ite  to download these programs in the target convert the ``.bin''-programs to a ``.s19''-format if using RS-232.
> 	\ei
> 	\os{\newpage}
> 	\ite Development in the target system
> 	\bi	
> 		\ite use of the same compiler (m68k-elf-gcc) as in the case with cross compilation.
> 		\ite the development has to be done in the target system (longer development cycles).
> 		\ite the executable file has to be generated position independent(controlled by a special linker command).
> 		\ite in the case of CFV4e-processor the MMU is active.
> 		\ite the output of the linker is build as .elf-file (elf - \underline{e}xecutable and \underline{l}inkable \underline{f}ormat) and could directly loaded from the operating system. 
> 	\ei
> 	\bigskip
> 	\ite Development with the same operating system in the target and in the cross-system
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite use of uLinux as operating system on the target.
> 		\ite uLinunx works with and without a MMU.
> 		\ite implementation of an NFS-server (\underline{n}etwork \underline{f}ile \underline{s}ystem) on the cross-system. 
> 		\ite mount the ``working directory'' of the cross-system in the target and direct load and execution is possible.
> 		\ite shorter development cycles as in the cross-compilation.
> 		\ite uses the advantages of an operating system.
> 	\ei
> \ei
> \os{\newpage}
> \item[{\rot\bf Special features}]~\\[-3ex]
> \bi 
> 	\ite no restriction in the use of data types.
> 	\ite the type float and double also supported (the basic arithmetic steps like add, sub, mul, and div directly supported from the FPU, more complex floating point arithmetic expressions like sin(x), cos(x), \begin{math} x^{y} \end{math} supported from a special library).
> 	\bigskip
> 	\ite access to registers (and memory) could be done in two different methods:
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite cross-compilation without the use on the MMU (independent of the OS, the memory here is managed by the complier).
> 		\ite set a pointer to the register (type of pointer depends on the register).
> 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		  \#define IPSBAR 0x1000000 & /* Base address of */ \\
> 					& /* I/O-registers */ \\
> 		  \#define PAR\_PCS3 (IPSBAR + 0xA4C) & /* Address of the specific */ \\
> 					& /* I/O */ \\
> 		    & \\
> 		  (*unsigned char *)(PAR\_PCS3)) = 0x00; & /* Set Register PAR\_PCS3 */ \\
> 					& /* to 0 */ \\
> 		   \end{tabular}
> 		\ite program runs in the ``user-mode'' of the processor.
> 		\bigskip
> 		\ite development in the target with the use of the MMU.
> 		\ite program have to be developed in the ``kernel-mode''.
> 	\ei
> 	
> \ei
> \os{\newpage}
> \item[{\rot\bf Parameter Passing Conventions}]~\\[-3ex]
> \bi 
> 	\ite C-Programs and Assembler-Programs could be combined without any restrictions.
> 	\ite this feature is based on a similar concept about passing parameters (C-parameter passing conventions).
> 	\bi 
> 		\ite specification of the return value of a function (always passed in registers D0 and FP0 as follows)
> 		\\ \begin{tabular}{l l}
> 		    Byte (char, unsigned char) & \pr D0[7..0] \\
> 		    Word (short, unsigned short) & \pr D0[15..0] \\
> 		    Long (int, unsigned int, pointers) & \pr D0 \\
> 		    Float & \pr FP0 \\
> 		    Double & \pr FP0 \\	
> 		   \end{tabular}
> \os{\newpage}
> 		\ite the calling parameter are passed on the stack (from left to right).e.g. int myfunc(int i, int j, int k);
> 		\newline
> 		\begin{center}
> 		Preparation of the Stack \\
> 		\begin{tabular}{c}
> 		   \\
> 		   \\
> 		   \\
> 		 SP \pr \\
> 		\end{tabular}
> 		\begin{tabular}{|c|}
> 		  \hline
> 		  int k \\
> 		  \hline
> 		  int j \\
> 		  \hline
> 		  int i \\
> 		  \hline
> 		  returnaddr \\
> 		  \hline
> 		\end{tabular}
> 		\begin{tabular}{c}
> 		  4 Byte \\
> 		  4 Byte \\
> 		  4 Byte \\
> 		  4 Byte \\
> 		\end{tabular}
> 		\end{center}
> 		\bigskip
> 		\ite if there are local variable necessary, they could placed under the return address.
> 
> 	\ei 
> \ei
> \end{description}
> 
